\begin{equation}\label{eq:opt_sp_trick}
    \min_{\vector{x}}\ \ L(\vector{x}) + \alpha_t R_1(\vector{x}) + \beta_t R_2(\vector{x}),
\end{equation}

\begin{align}
\dfrac{\alpha_t R_1(\vector{x}_t)}{L(\vector{x}_t)} = w_\alpha &\equiv \mbox{const}, \label{eq:alpha}\\
\dfrac{\beta_t R_2(\vector{x}_t)}{L(\vector{x}_t)} = w_\beta &\equiv \mbox{const}. \label{eq:beta}
\end{align}

Formally, the $i$-th output weight vector is estimated by
\begin{equation}
\hat{\vector{W}}_i = \vector{D} \vector{x}_i, \label{eq:sp2detector}
\end{equation}
The biases can also be compressed as
\begin{equation}
\hat{b}_i = \vector{c} \vector{x}_i. \label{eq:sp2detector_bias}
\end{equation}
where $\vector{x}_i$ is the sparse representation of the $i$-th word. (It is shared in the compression of weights and biases.)

In the above model, we have managed to compressed a language model whose number of parameters is
irrelevant to the vocabulary size.

